---
import "../../styles/homepage.css";
import "../../styles/puzzlequests.css";
const year = new Date().getFullYear();
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>Puzzle Quests &mdash; The Lions Alliance</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Puzzle Quests is a family of hand-built heritage walking experiences by The Lions Alliance. The Skeleton Key — our first quest — is in prototype and headed for Museum Medelo in Meijel." />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://thelionsalliance.com/puzzlequests/" />
    <link rel="alternate" hreflang="nl" href="https://thelionsalliance.com/puzzlequests/nl/" />
    <link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://thelionsalliance.com/puzzlequests/" />
  </head>
  <body class="tla-page pl-body">

    <!-- ── Navigation ─────────────────────────────────── -->
    <nav class="tla-nav" id="tla-nav">
      <div class="tla-nav__inner tla-container">
        <a href="/" class="tla-nav__logo">The Lions <span>Alliance</span></a>
        <ul class="tla-nav__links">
          <li><a href="#overview">Overview</a></li>
          <li><a href="#pilot">Meijel Pilot</a></li>
          <li><a href="#skeleton-key">Skeleton Key</a></li>
          <li><a href="#adapt">For Your Venue</a></li>
        </ul>
        <div class="tla-nav__actions">
          <a href="/puzzlequests/nl/" class="tla-nav__lang" hreflang="nl" lang="nl" title="Bekijk in het Nederlands">NL</a>
          <a href="/contact/" class="tla-nav__cta tla-nav__cta--gold">Contact</a>
          <a href="/" class="tla-nav__cta">&larr; Studio</a>
        </div>
        <button class="tla-nav__hamburger" id="hamburger" aria-label="Toggle menu">
          <span></span><span></span><span></span>
        </button>
      </div>
      <div class="tla-nav__mobile" id="mobile-menu">
        <a href="#overview">Overview</a>
        <a href="#pilot">Meijel Pilot</a>
        <a href="#skeleton-key">Skeleton Key</a>
        <a href="#adapt">For Your Venue</a>
        <a href="/contact/">Contact</a>
        <a href="/puzzlequests/nl/" hreflang="nl" lang="nl">Bekijk in het Nederlands</a>
        <a href="/">&larr; Back to Studio</a>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <!-- ── Hero ───────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="pl-hero" id="home">
      <div class="tla-container pl-hero__inner">
        <div class="pl-hero__text tla-fade">
          <div class="pl-hero__breadcrumb">
            <a href="/">The Lions Alliance</a>
            <span class="pl-hero__breadcrumb-sep">/</span>
            <span>Experiences Pillar</span>
          </div>
          <div class="pl-hero__badges">
            <span class="tla-badge tla-badge--built">Prototype Ready</span>
            <span class="tla-badge tla-badge--dev">In Development</span>
            <span class="tla-badge tla-badge--live">Heritage Pilot 2026</span>
          </div>
          <h1 class="pl-hero__title">Puzzle <em>Quests</em></h1>
          <p class="pl-hero__subtitle">
            The Skeleton Key &mdash; our first hand-built heritage walking experience &mdash; is in prototype at our studio, with the lion&rsquo;s head printed, the components soldered, and the shell being assembled. A local legend carried in the visitor&rsquo;s hand, as a bronze key that responds to hidden lock stations along the route. No app, no screen, no host required.
          </p>
          <div class="pl-hero__ctas">
            <a href="#collaborate" class="tla-btn tla-btn--gold">Bring this to your venue</a>
            <a href="#skeleton-key" class="tla-btn tla-btn--outline">See the Skeleton Key &darr;</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pl-hero__visual tla-fade tla-d1">
          <figure class="pl-render">
            <img src="/images/puzzle-quests/skeleton-key/sk-unlit.webp" alt="The Skeleton Key prop — an ornate aged-bronze lion's-head bow, engraved shaft with hidden rune positions, and heraldic NFC bit, on a dark studio background. Prototype render." loading="eager" />
          </figure>
          <span class="pl-render__caption">The Skeleton Key &mdash; prototype render &middot; <em>De Vlucht door de Peel</em></span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── The Concept ────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="pl-overview" id="overview">
      <div class="tla-container pl-overview__inner">
        <div class="pl-overview__text tla-fade">
          <span class="tla-overline">The concept</span>
          <h2 class="tla-section-header pl-section-header--left">A heritage story, walked through the landscape that holds it</h2>
          <p>
            Puzzle Quests is a family of hand-built walking experiences. Each starts and ends at the partner museum&rsquo;s reception desk and follows a route through the surrounding village, peatland, or heritage site. As the visitor walks, the prop in their hands responds to the world around them &mdash; lighting up, vibrating, writing itself, illuminating hidden details.
          </p>
          <p>
            Each quest is built around a specific local legend or archival story. The story is the spine of the experience; the prop is the way the visitor enters it. We design everything from the ground up: the narrative, the prop, the route, the booklet, the puzzle, the on-site stations, the staff briefing at the museum desk.
          </p>
          <p>
            The first three instances are being designed and built for <strong>Museum Medelo in Meijel</strong>, in partnership with Heemkundevereniging Medelo. The hardware, the puzzle frameworks, and the operational model are designed for replication &mdash; once the Meijel pilot proves the format, the same props adapt to other regional museums and heritage venues with a story-research effort measured in tens of hours, not years.
          </p>
          <div class="pl-tags">
            <span class="pl-tag">Museums &amp; heritage</span>
            <span class="pl-tag">Local-legend storytelling</span>
            <span class="pl-tag">Walking routes</span>
            <span class="pl-tag">School programmes</span>
            <span class="pl-tag">Revenue-share model</span>
            <span class="pl-tag">No app, no host</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pl-overview__visual tla-fade tla-d1">
          <figure class="pl-sketch">
            <img src="/images/puzzle-quests/Concept_WorkingDesk.png" alt="Studio working-desk sketchbook page showing the archive folder from Heemkundevereniging Medelo, a researcher's hand writing, three prop-design sheets stacked at the corner, and a process timeline from archive to museum desk." loading="lazy" />
          </figure>
          <span class="pl-sketch__caption">Working desk &mdash; from het Medelo-archief to a walked route</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="tla-divider"></div>

    <!-- ── Meijel Pilot ───────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="pl-pilot" id="pilot">
      <div class="tla-container">
        <div class="tla-fade" style="text-align:center;">
          <span class="tla-overline">First pilot</span>
          <h2 class="tla-section-header">Museum Medelo, Meijel</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="pl-pilot__grid">
          <div class="pl-pilot__text tla-fade">
            <p>
              The first three Puzzle Quests are being designed and built in partnership with <strong>Heemkundevereniging Medelo</strong> &mdash; the local-history society that runs Museum Medelo at Tomveld 2 in Meijel. Each quest is rooted in a real archival story from the Medelo collection: a flight from Prussian recruiters in the 1730s, the life of a Belgian vagrant who walked the Peel for forty years, and a Roman legionary&rsquo;s remains found in the peat near the Meijel&ndash;Sevenum road.
            </p>
            <p>
              The Medelo partnership gives us three things at once: archival depth that makes each story credible, a starting venue where visitors pick up the kit and return to a stamp at the end, and an operating track record that supports approaching other regional museums (Limburgs Museum, Streekmuseum De Locht, and the fortified-town partners across Brabant and Limburg).
            </p>
            <p>
              Our goal for 2026 is to take the Skeleton Key from prop prototype to live museum operation. The other two quests follow on the validated kit and operational pattern.
            </p>
          </div>
          <ul class="pl-pilot__facts tla-fade tla-d1">
            <li><span class="pl-pilot__label">Venue</span><span class="pl-pilot__value">Museum Medelo, Tomveld 2, Meijel</span></li>
            <li><span class="pl-pilot__label">Heritage partner</span><span class="pl-pilot__value">Heemkundevereniging Medelo</span></li>
            <li><span class="pl-pilot__label">First quest live</span><span class="pl-pilot__value">Target late 2026</span></li>
            <li><span class="pl-pilot__label">Format</span><span class="pl-pilot__value">Walking route, 45&ndash;95 min, return to museum desk</span></li>
            <li><span class="pl-pilot__label">Audiences</span><span class="pl-pilot__value">Family visits &middot; school groups (90&ndash;120 min) &middot; couples</span></li>
            <li><span class="pl-pilot__label">Operating model</span><span class="pl-pilot__value">Revenue-share with the host museum</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="tla-divider"></div>

    <!-- ── How It Works ───────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="pl-steps-section" id="how-it-works">
      <div class="tla-container">
        <div class="tla-fade" style="text-align:center;">
          <span class="tla-overline">How it works</span>
          <h2 class="tla-section-header">The visitor journey, in three beats</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="pl-steps">
          <div class="pl-step tla-fade">
            <div class="pl-step__number">I</div>
            <div class="pl-step__title">Collect the kit</div>
            <div class="pl-step__desc">At the museum desk: a prop, a booklet, a route notesheet, and a short briefing in the story. No app, no screen, no setup.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="pl-step tla-fade tla-d1">
            <div class="pl-step__number">II</div>
            <div class="pl-step__title">Walk the route</div>
            <div class="pl-step__desc">The route runs through the village and surrounding landscape. The prop responds at every station &mdash; through lock, page, or lantern &mdash; revealing one more piece of the story.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="pl-step tla-fade tla-d2">
            <div class="pl-step__number">III</div>
            <div class="pl-step__title">Return to the desk</div>
            <div class="pl-step__desc">Visitors return with a completed booklet and the prop lit up with their progress. Staff verify, stamp the notesheet, and use the conversation to share an extra archival detail. The kit resets in under five minutes for the next group.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Skeleton Key (flagship) ───────────────────── -->
    <section class="pl-quests" id="skeleton-key">
      <div class="tla-container">
        <div class="tla-fade" style="text-align:center;">
          <span class="tla-overline">Quest I &middot; In fabrication</span>
          <h2 class="tla-section-header">The Skeleton Key</h2>
          <p class="pl-quests__intro">The first quest has left the design stage. The 3D model is printed, the electronics are soldered, and the lion&rsquo;s head is being assembled. Here is what a visitor will pick up at the museum desk.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- ── The object ──────────────────────────── -->
        <article class="pl-quest pl-quest--key tla-fade" id="quest-key">
          <div class="pl-quest__visual">
            <figure class="pl-render pl-render--blend">
              <img src="/images/puzzle-quests/skeleton-key/sk-head.webp" alt="Closeup render of the Skeleton Key's lion's-head bow — an ornate bronze frame with acanthus-leaf scrollwork surrounding a detailed lion's face, circa-1730 heraldic style." loading="lazy" />
            </figure>
            <span class="pl-render__caption">The Skeleton Key &mdash; lion&rsquo;s-head bow, prototype render &middot; version I</span>
          </div>
          <div class="pl-quest__body">
            <div class="pl-quest__eyebrow">Quest I &middot; Skeleton Key</div>
            <h3 class="pl-quest__title">De Vlucht door de Peel</h3>
            <p class="pl-quest__story">
              Between 1713 and 1740, Prussian King Frederick Wilhelm I sent recruiters across the border to collect exceptionally tall young men for his elite Riesengarde. The men who fled used a secret network of safe-houses through the Peel. Those who made it to Meijel were free &mdash; they settled, married, and had tall children. This is documented as the reason Meijel has historically more tall men than its neighbouring villages.
            </p>
            <p>
              The Skeleton Key is the warden&rsquo;s master pass. Eight genuine lock-stations &mdash; hidden in tree trunks, mossy stones, and trail-posts along the route &mdash; each carry one letter of the keyword <strong>VRIJHEID</strong> (Freedom). The key senses each next station via radio proximity, pulsing harder as the walker closes in. At each lock the key is physically turned, an NFC-scan triggers, and one of the runes along the shaft glows softly through the aged brass. By the final lock all eight runes are lit. The walker speaks the keyword aloud at the museum desk &mdash; that is their proof of passage.
            </p>
            <ul class="pl-quest__chips">
              <li><span>Difficulty</span><strong>&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9734;</strong></li>
              <li><span>Duration</span><strong>75&ndash;95 min</strong></li>
              <li><span>Distance</span><strong>~5 km</strong></li>
              <li><span>Group</span><strong>2&ndash;6</strong></li>
              <li><span>Age</span><strong>10+</strong></li>
              <li><span>Mechanic</span><strong>Chain of locks &middot; radio proximity &middot; 8-letter word</strong></li>
            </ul>
            <div class="pl-quest__status">
              <span class="pl-quest__status-dot"></span>
              <span>Prototype fabricated &middot; Two resin prints complete &middot; Electronic components soldered &middot; Shell assembly in progress</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ── Lock stations ────────────────────────── -->
        <article class="pl-quest pl-quest--key-stations tla-fade tla-d1">
          <div class="pl-quest__visual pl-quest__visual--wide">
            <figure class="pl-sketch">
              <img src="/images/puzzle-quests/SkeletonKey_Stations.png" alt="Three lock-station archetypes for the Skeleton Key quest — tree trunk, mossy stone, and Dutch trail-post — each hiding a keyhole with reactive vein-lighting." loading="lazy" />
            </figure>
            <span class="pl-sketch__caption">Lock-station archetypes &mdash; tree-trunk, stone, trail-post; invisible to passers-by, alive to the key</span>
          </div>
          <div class="pl-quest__body pl-quest__body--narrow">
            <p>
              Stations are designed to disappear into the landscape. A casual passer-by walks past them without noticing &mdash; only the visitor carrying the key sees the hidden veins of light begin to pulse as they close in. Three archetypes distribute across the route: cast bark on a real tree, a Jesmonite-cast glacial-erratic stone, and a Dutch recreational trail-post. One additional station &mdash; the recruiter&rsquo;s decoy &mdash; lures rushed players who ignore the colour chain.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Interactive Rune Walk ─────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="pl-walk" id="rune-walk">
      <div class="tla-container">
        <div class="tla-fade" style="text-align:center;">
          <span class="tla-overline">The mechanic, in motion</span>
          <h2 class="tla-section-header">Each lock reveals a rune</h2>
          <p class="pl-walk__intro">Drag the walker along the route. At each station the key image updates and one more Peelschrift rune glows along the shaft. Placeholder glyphs &mdash; Daniel will supply the actual Peelschrift alphabet when the design is finalised.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tla-fade">
          <div class="pl-walk__display">
            <figure class="pl-walk__key-fig">
              <img id="walk-key-img" class="pl-walk__key-img"
                   src="/images/puzzle-quests/skeleton-key/sk-unlit.webp"
                   alt="The Skeleton Key — rune state updates as the walker progresses along the route"
                   loading="lazy" />
            </figure>
            <div class="pl-walk__runes" aria-live="polite" aria-label="Rune progression">
              <span class="pl-walk__rune" data-rune="&#5792;">&#9676;</span>
              <span class="pl-walk__rune" data-rune="&#5794;">&#9676;</span>
              <span class="pl-walk__rune" data-rune="&#5798;">&#9676;</span>
              <span class="pl-walk__rune" data-rune="&#5800;">&#9676;</span>
              <span class="pl-walk__rune" data-rune="&#5809;">&#9676;</span>
              <span class="pl-walk__rune" data-rune="&#5810;">&#9676;</span>
              <span class="pl-walk__rune" data-rune="&#5825;">&#9676;</span>
              <span class="pl-walk__rune" data-rune="&#5848;">&#9676;</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="pl-walk__stage-wrap">
            <svg id="walk-svg" class="pl-walk__svg" viewBox="0 0 900 240" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"
                 role="img" aria-label="Interactive walking route — drag to progress through stations">
              <defs>
                <symbol id="icon-rock" viewBox="-15 -16 30 24" overflow="visible">
                  <path d="M-10,4 C-13,-7 -7,-16 0,-14 C7,-12 13,-7 11,4 C9,11 -8,11 -10,4Z"
                        fill="#9B8B78" stroke="#7A6A5A" stroke-width="0.7"/>
                  <path d="M-5,-7 C-2,-12 3,-11 5,-7" stroke="#C0B098" stroke-width="0.8" fill="none" opacity="0.55"/>
                </symbol>
                <symbol id="icon-stump" viewBox="-12 -16 24 24" overflow="visible">
                  <rect x="-9" y="-7" width="18" height="13" rx="1" fill="#7A5A42"/>
                  <ellipse cx="0" cy="-7" rx="9" ry="3.5" fill="#6A4A35"/>
                  <ellipse cx="0" cy="6"  rx="9" ry="3"   fill="#6A4A35"/>
                  <path d="M-3,-7 L-2,-14 M3,-7 L3,-13 M7,-4 L9,-10"
                        stroke="#9B7050" stroke-width="1.2" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round"/>
                </symbol>
                <symbol id="icon-pole" viewBox="-8 -24 16 32" overflow="visible">
                  <rect x="-2.5" y="-22" width="5" height="28" rx="1.5" fill="#8B7352"/>
                  <ellipse cx="0" cy="-22" rx="6"  ry="2.5" fill="#A08860"/>
                  <ellipse cx="0" cy="-22" rx="4"  ry="1.8" fill="#C0A878"/>
                </symbol>
              </defs>

              <!-- Subtle ground line -->
              <line x1="0" y1="160" x2="900" y2="160" stroke="rgba(176,141,87,0.07)" stroke-width="1"/>

              <!-- Winding dashed route path -->
              <path id="walk-path"
                    d="M 20,150 C 80,90 170,195 250,150 S 400,90 470,150 S 600,195 680,150 S 820,90 880,150"
                    stroke="rgba(176,141,87,0.38)"
                    stroke-width="3"
                    stroke-dasharray="8 6"
                    fill="none"
                    stroke-linecap="round"/>

              <!-- Walker and station elements added by JS -->
            </svg>
            <p class="pl-walk__hint">&#8592; drag the walker along the route &#8594;</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Other quest formats ────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="pl-formats" id="other-formats">
      <div class="tla-container">
        <div class="tla-fade" style="text-align:center;">
          <span class="tla-overline">Other formats</span>
          <h2 class="tla-section-header">Two more quest frameworks, fully designed</h2>
          <p class="pl-formats__intro">Not every venue calls for a key. We&rsquo;ve designed two additional prop formats &mdash; each with a complete narrative, build guide, and puzzle framework &mdash; for locations whose heritage story calls for something different.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pl-format-grid">

          <!-- Living Chronicle -->
          <div class="pl-format-card tla-fade" id="quest-chronicle">
            <div class="pl-format-card__img">
              <img src="/images/puzzle-quests/LivingChronicle.png" alt="Design page of the Living Chronicle prop — a forest-green leather grimoire with embossed tree-and-bog cover and seven flexible e-paper pages." loading="lazy" />
            </div>
            <div class="pl-format-card__body">
              <div class="pl-format-card__eyebrow">
                Quest II &middot; Living Chronicle
                <span class="pl-format-card__concept-badge">Concept</span>
              </div>
              <div class="pl-format-card__title">Living Chronicle</div>
              <div class="pl-format-card__subtitle"><em>Het Pad van Dikke Mie</em></div>
              <p class="pl-format-card__desc">
                A leather-bound tome with seven flexible e-paper pages, all blank at the start except for one inscription. At each Memorial Circle on the route, the visitor places the closed book on an engraved disk &mdash; the book trembles, the spine glows faintly, and a new page writes itself. By the end of the walk the visitor holds a complete hand-walked chronicle of a forgotten life. Best suited to memorial or biographical heritage stories with a defined walking route.
              </p>
              <div class="pl-format-card__chips">
                <span class="pl-format-card__chip">&#9733;&#9733;&#9734;&#9734; Difficulty</span>
                <span class="pl-format-card__chip">45&ndash;60 min</span>
                <span class="pl-format-card__chip">~2 km</span>
                <span class="pl-format-card__chip">14+</span>
                <span class="pl-format-card__chip">Seven self-writing pages</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Puzzle Lantern -->
          <div class="pl-format-card tla-fade tla-d1" id="quest-lantern">
            <div class="pl-format-card__img">
              <img src="/images/puzzle-quests/Lantern.png" alt="Design page of the Puzzle Lantern prop — a four-sided verdigris green-bronze Victorian-style lantern with ornate roof, dormer UV window, brass handle and four legs." loading="lazy" />
            </div>
            <div class="pl-format-card__body">
              <div class="pl-format-card__eyebrow">
                Quest III &middot; Puzzle Lantern
                <span class="pl-format-card__concept-badge">Concept</span>
              </div>
              <div class="pl-format-card__title">Puzzle Lantern</div>
              <div class="pl-format-card__subtitle"><em>De Schat van het Dolle Moer</em></div>
              <p class="pl-format-card__desc">
                A verdigris Victorian lantern that reads NFC station posts and lights its ring with coordinates &mdash; decoded by the visitor against a 13-page surveyor&rsquo;s journal in their other hand. Pages fold along marked lines to align Peelschrift glyphs, layer with translucent vellum, and reveal hidden symbols under the lantern&rsquo;s dormer UV beam. The hardest of the three formats; best suited to archaeological or mystery-treasure narratives.
              </p>
              <div class="pl-format-card__chips">
                <span class="pl-format-card__chip">&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9733; Difficulty</span>
                <span class="pl-format-card__chip">60&ndash;90 min</span>
                <span class="pl-format-card__chip">~3&ndash;4 km</span>
                <span class="pl-format-card__chip">10+</span>
                <span class="pl-format-card__chip">Lantern + journal &middot; UV reveal</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Adapt for your venue ───────────────────────── -->
    <section class="pl-adapt" id="adapt">
      <div class="tla-container">
        <div class="tla-fade" style="text-align:center;">
          <span class="tla-overline">For your venue</span>
          <h2 class="tla-section-header">The hardware is reusable. The story is the work.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="pl-adapt__grid">
          <div class="pl-adapt__col tla-fade">
            <h3 class="pl-adapt__title">What stays fixed</h3>
            <ul class="pl-adapt__list">
              <li>The prop hardware &mdash; we build it once and re-use it across instances.</li>
              <li>The puzzle frameworks (chain navigation, memorial page-reveal, lantern coordinate decode).</li>
              <li>The operating model &mdash; pickup at desk, walk the route, return to desk for the stamp and conversation.</li>
              <li>The technical platform &mdash; NFC, radio proximity, e-paper, UV-reveal.</li>
              <li>The five-minute kit reset between groups.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="pl-adapt__col tla-fade tla-d1">
            <h3 class="pl-adapt__title">What we design per venue</h3>
            <ul class="pl-adapt__list">
              <li>The story &mdash; researched from your archive, written for the route.</li>
              <li>Illustrations, booklet content, and any cipher tied to your venue.</li>
              <li>The route &mdash; scouted on-site, station placement, length, walking grade.</li>
              <li>Station object aesthetics adapted to your landscape (cast iron beam, stone, bronze plaque, etc.).</li>
              <li>Staff briefing for the desk conversation at return.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="pl-adapt__effort">
              Adaptation effort per quest: <strong>20&ndash;50 hours of design work</strong>, depending on the quest type. The prop hardware is reused as-is; only engraved details, paint colourways, firmware constants, and content are venue-specific. Not every location calls for a key &mdash; if your story is better told through a book or a lantern, we can talk about that too.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

