    <!-- ── Our Thinking ───────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="pl-philosophy">
      <div class="tla-container">
        <div class="tla-fade" style="text-align:center;">
          <span class="tla-overline">Our thinking</span>
          <h2 class="tla-section-header">Why we are building this</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="pl-philosophy__grid">
          <div class="pl-philosophy__card tla-fade">
            <span class="pl-philosophy__card-icon">&#9995;</span>
            <div class="pl-philosophy__card-title">Tactile over purely digital</div>
            <div class="pl-philosophy__card-desc">Real physical interaction creates moments that screens cannot replicate &mdash; weight, light, vibration, and the surprise of an object that responds to you become part of the story.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="pl-philosophy__card tla-fade tla-d1">
            <span class="pl-philosophy__card-icon">&#129309;</span>
            <div class="pl-philosophy__card-title">Heritage as living material</div>
            <div class="pl-philosophy__card-desc">Local archives hold stories that no screen can do justice to. We work directly with the heritage society of each venue to ensure the legend on the page is the legend that was actually walked.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="pl-philosophy__card tla-fade tla-d2">
            <span class="pl-philosophy__card-icon">&#127761;</span>
            <div class="pl-philosophy__card-title">Atmosphere as a design material</div>
            <div class="pl-philosophy__card-desc">Light, weight, sound, and the timing of a reveal are deliberate tools. Each prop is engineered to feel like it belongs in the century where the story takes place.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="pl-philosophy__card tla-fade tla-d3">
            <span class="pl-philosophy__card-icon">&#127759;</span>
            <div class="pl-philosophy__card-title">The desk conversation matters</div>
            <div class="pl-philosophy__card-desc">Every quest ends with the visitor returning to the museum desk for a stamp. We design that conversation: staff are briefed not just to check answers, but to share one more archival detail. That conversation is the highest-value moment a museum has with each visitor.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Collaboration CTA ──────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="pl-collaborate" id="collaborate">
      <div class="tla-container pl-collaborate__inner">
        <div class="pl-collaborate__text tla-fade">
          <span class="tla-overline">Partnership</span>
          <h2 class="tla-section-header pl-section-header--left">Bring a Puzzle Quest to your museum</h2>
          <p>
            We&rsquo;re looking for the next two or three regional museums and heritage venues to deploy Puzzle Quests after the Meijel pilot completes. If you have a local story that deserves to be walked &mdash; a forgotten figure, a documented escape, an archaeological find, a folk legend with a real archival trace &mdash; we&rsquo;d love to hear from you.
          </p>
          <p>
            The model is a revenue-share on net ticket revenue, with the museum hosting the kit, the briefing, and the return conversation. We handle story research, prop adaptation, route scouting, on-site testing, and staff training. The first conversation is usually a visit to your venue, an hour with your archivist, and a walk of one possible route.
          </p>
          <div class="pl-collaborate__ctas">
            <a href="/contact/" class="tla-btn tla-btn--gold">Get in touch</a>
            <a href="/" class="tla-btn tla-btn--outline">Explore the studio &rarr;</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pl-collaborate__audiences tla-fade tla-d1">
          <ul class="pl-audience-list">
            <li class="pl-audience-item"><span class="pl-audience-dot"></span>Regional &amp; local-history museums</li>
            <li class="pl-audience-item"><span class="pl-audience-dot"></span>Heritage societies &amp; archives</li>
            <li class="pl-audience-item"><span class="pl-audience-dot"></span>Castle &amp; fortified-town visitor centres</li>
            <li class="pl-audience-item"><span class="pl-audience-dot"></span>National parks &amp; landscape trusts</li>
            <li class="pl-audience-item"><span class="pl-audience-dot"></span>Municipal heritage &amp; tourism offices</li>
            <li class="pl-audience-item"><span class="pl-audience-dot"></span>School programme partners</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── FAQ ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="pl-faq">
      <div class="tla-container">
        <div class="tla-fade" style="text-align:center;">
          <span class="tla-overline">FAQ</span>
          <h2 class="tla-section-header">Common questions from museum partners</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="pl-faq__list">
          <div class="pl-faq__item tla-fade">
            <div class="pl-faq__question">How does the operating model work in practice?</div>
            <div class="pl-faq__answer">Visitors purchase the quest at the museum reception (we suggest &euro;8&ndash;15 per student for school programmes, scaled for families and adults). The museum keeps a 30&ndash;40% share of net ticket revenue. The kit is reusable and resets in under five minutes between groups. Staff need a short briefing per quest to handle the return-desk conversation; we provide that briefing as part of the launch.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="pl-faq__item tla-fade tla-d1">
            <div class="pl-faq__question">What does adapting a quest to our venue actually involve?</div>
            <div class="pl-faq__answer">The hardware is reused unchanged. The custom work per venue is: story research (with your archivist), narrative writing, illustration, station-object aesthetics adapted to your landscape, route scouting, and staff briefing. Effort ranges from 20 to 50 hours of design work per quest, depending on which of the three you start with. We start with one quest, validate the operation, then add the others as the partnership grows.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="pl-faq__item tla-fade tla-d2">
            <div class="pl-faq__question">Do visitors need a smartphone or an app?</div>
            <div class="pl-faq__answer">No. Every quest is fully self-contained in the physical prop and booklet. The prop has its own NFC reader, e-paper, lighting, and audio &mdash; the visitor&rsquo;s phone stays in their pocket the entire walk.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="pl-faq__item tla-fade tla-d3">
            <div class="pl-faq__question">How does it handle weather?</div>
            <div class="pl-faq__answer">Props are designed for Peel weather &mdash; sealed leather covers, waterproof page materials, exterior-grade varnishes on station objects. Acceptable exposure is light rain and mist. For prolonged downpour or freezing conditions, the quest can be paused at the museum&rsquo;s discretion.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="pl-faq__item tla-fade">
            <div class="pl-faq__question">When can we book a pilot for our venue?</div>
            <div class="pl-faq__answer">The Meijel pilot is the first live deployment, targeted for late 2026. Second-venue conversations begin in parallel; the first new-venue rollout would realistically be 2027. Early conversations now are how we shape the next year of work &mdash; the earlier we know your story, the sooner it can become a quest.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

